 poset always gives a natural labeling of the poset. A labeling is called tangled if it takes the full $n-1$ promotions to become a natural labeling. They characterized posets that have tangled labelings and enumerated the tangled labelings of a certain class of posets. They conjectured that the number of tangled labelings of any $n$-element poset is at most $(n-1)!$. Hodges, in his paper \\textit{On Promotion and Quasi-tangled Labelings of Posets}, refined the conjecture to lower the upper bound based on the number of minimal elements of the poset. We show that Hodges’ upper bound holds for two classes of posets, inflated rooted forest posets and shoelace posets, and investigate a method of further characterizing these tangled labelings using generating functions.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn Promotion and Quasi-tangled Labelings of Posets (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/362789986_On_Promotion_and_Quasi-tangled_Labelings_of_Posets)\nciteturn0search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; In addition to computing the sizes of the fibers of promotion for rooted tree posets, we count the quasi-tangled labelings of a relatively large class of posets called inflated rooted trees with deflated leaves. ... We also make partial progress towards proving a conjecture of Defant and Kravitz on the maximum possible number of tangled labelings of an n-element poset.\n\n# On Promotion and Quasi-tangled Labelings of Posets\n\n  * August 2022\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.2208.08665\n\nAuthors:\n\nEliot Hodges\n\nEliot Hodges\n\n\n## Abstract\n\nIn 2022, Defant and Kravitz introduced extended promotion (denoted $\\partial$), a map that acts on the set of labelings of a poset. Extended promotion is a generalization of Sch\\\"{u}tzenberger's promotion operator, a well-studied map that permutes the set of linear extensions of a poset. It is known that if L is a labeling of an n-element poset P, then $\\partial^{n-1}(L)$ is a linear extension. This allows us to regard $\\partial$ as a sorting operator on the set of all labelings of P, where we think of the linear extensions of P as the labelings which have been sorted. The labelings requiring $n-1$ applications of $\\partial$ to be sorted are called tangled; the labelings requiring $n-2$ applications are called quasi-tangled. In addition to computing the sizes of the fibers of promotion for rooted tree posets, we count the quasi-tangled labelings of a relatively large class of posets called inflated rooted trees with deflated leaves. Given an n-element poset with a unique minimal element with the property that the minimal element has exactly one parent, it follows from the aforementioned enumeration that this poset has $2(n-1)!-(n-2)!$ quasi-tangled labelings. Using similar methods, we outline an algorithmic approach to enumerating the labelings requiring $n-k-1$ applications to be sorted for any fixed $k\\in\\{1,\\ldots,n-2\\}$. We also make partial progress towards proving a conjecture of Defant and Kravitz on the maximum possible number of tangled labelings of an n-element poset.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSeattle, January 8–11, 2025 (https://www.ams.org/journals/abs/2025-46-01/1203.pdf)\nciteturn0search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Promotion and Quasi-tangled Labelings of Posets}, refined the conjecture to lower the ... Hodges’ upper bound holds for two classes of posets, inflated rooted forest posets and\nThey characterized posets that have tangled labelings and enumerated the tangled\nlabelings of a certain class of posets. They conjectured that the number of tangled\nlabelings of any $n$-element poset is at most $(n-1)!$. Hodges, in his paper \\textit{On\nPromotion and Quasi-tangled Labelings of Posets}, refined the conjecture to lower the\nupper bound based on the number of minimal elements of the poset. We show that\nHodges’ upper bound holds for two classes of